  organizational effectiveness and the  reason I say that is because  organizations are defined by  coordinating people's efforts otherwise  we're just a craft person a doc alone a  doc in a healthcare system is connected  to other people and the capacity of that  system to be effective is I think  related to the capacity tax system to  access information about what's going on  and then to make decisions about how to  improve or change those things but I'm a  little confused because if you really  say it's about having everybody involved  with the task able to say what they  think and feel about the task or about  each other at all times and couldn't  that really get a team into pretty  difficult territory if people really say  what they think and feel at all times so  that it it's crucial when I say about  the task I'm actually not saying about  each other  unless there's a reaction to each other  that that that relates to the task so  for example it's the in the classic  example about the challenge of doing  this is the effect of for example  hierarchy on the capacity of an  organization to do this so that the  expression that it's hard to deliver bad  news up the hierarchy from my point of  view is a statement about the  effectiveness of the organization and if  you ask people in senior roles they'll  say one of my biggest challenges is  getting people to tell me what they  actually think especially when what they  think is critical of what they imagine  to be my goal or my objective and that  puts the in this case the senior person  at risk for making a bad decision or for  coming up with a poor strategy because  people who have other sources of  information are unable unwilling feel  that they can't say what they're  thinking and feeling especially as it  relates to up the hierarchy that's  usually Nega  thought or feeling so but it's about the  task it's not John I really don't like  you it's John I think you're you're  you're not you don't have all the  information about X and I wouldn't have  done Y if I had all the information let  me tell you what the information is that  I have so let's take this from you  introduce the idea of hierarchy so let's  take this from being the person at the  top of the hierarchy and then being  people who are really following that so  first being at the top of the hierarchy  what can you really do I mean what can  you do if people sometimes just don't  have the courage to tell you what's  going on and you all of a sudden realize  that patients have been waiting for  hours in the emergency room no one told  you you have no idea so I don't know if  I ever would have thought of that as an  organizational problem don't most people  think of that as somebody on the team  just has a problem they're not able to  tell you the truth yep that that is the  way most people think about it  I don't think about if you frame it as  courage you're framing it as an  individual characteristic and now you're  trying to select people who are  courageous but most of our evidence  right this umberto experiment so Philip  Zimbardo they just made a recent movie  about this did an experiment at Stanford  where he randomly assigned students to  roles of guards and inmates and what  essentially was disturbing about it and  not the first thing but because you  don't usually do a role play like this  that goes on for the schedule to go on  for a week and goes on for three days  and has to be stopped  because of how powerfully people take on  the roles what we learned from this is  that this is going to be a slight  overstatement but you let me control the  conditions I can probably get anybody to  do anything  which means courage is probably a  reflection of the social structure  that's been created in the organization  rather than a characteristic of the  person if someone is behaving  courageously or more broadly if you want  most of the people in your organization  to behave courageously  you're probably going to 